**New evidence**
1. Presidency speech on the economic programme
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"We have built our economic programme on our country’s growth through investment, employment, production, exports and current account surplus","publisher":"Republic of Türkiye Directorate of Communications","publication_date":"2022-09-22","url":"https://www.iletisim.gov.tr/english/haberler/detay/we-have-built-our-economic-programme-on-our-countrys-growth-through-investment-employment-production-exports-and-current-account-surplus","claim":"Erdoğan said the economic programme was deliberately built on growth through investment, employment, production, exports and current account surplus, and that he continued to implement it determinedly.","locator":"lines 53-59","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential speech on the presidency website; strong for declared goals and program framing, but self-report for execution quality.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Within 2022; explicit program statement in the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Erdoğan as his own stated governing program; implementation still partly mediated by ministries and the private sector.","contrary_evidence":["Self-described goals do not prove effective delivery.","Later IMF/OECD assessments judged the policy mix distortionary and inflationary."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.6"]}

2. 2022 annual program
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Yılı Cumhurbaşkanlığı Yıllık Programı 25/10/2021 Tarih ve 31639 (Mükerrer) Sayılı Resmi Gazete’de yayımlanmıştır","publisher":"T.C. Cumhurbaşkanlığı Strateji ve Bütçe Başkanlığı","publication_date":"2021-10-25","url":"https://www.sbb.gov.tr/2022-yili-cumhurbaskanligi-yillik-programi-25-10-2021-tarih-ve-31639-mukerrer-sayili-resmi-gazetede-yayimlanmistir/?device=1","claim":"The 2022 annual program set macroeconomic, fiscal and sectoral policies and said the implementation measures would be concrete, measurable, limited to one year, and reviewed quarterly.","locator":"search result snippet","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government program page; strong for formal priorities and implementation design, but not proof of execution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Pre-2022 program governing 2022 execution.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Erdoğan's presidential system and cabinet chain, though day-to-day implementation ran through ministries and agencies.","contrary_evidence":["A written program is not evidence of effective delivery.","Later macro outcomes show mixed or weak implementation."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.5","8B.6"]}

3. 2022 investment program
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Yılı Yatırım Programı Yayımlandı","publisher":"T.C. Cumhurbaşkanlığı Strateji ve Bütçe Başkanlığı","publication_date":"2022-01-15","url":"https://www.sbb.gov.tr/2022-yili-yatirim-programi-yayimlandi/?device=1","claim":"The 2022 investment program, signed by Erdoğan, allocated 184.3 billion TL to 3,134 projects and prioritized ongoing projects closest to completion and projects with the highest economic and social contribution and urgent service needs.","locator":"search result snippet","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official investment-program announcement with concrete allocations and selection criteria; strong for planned mobilization, weaker for actual completion.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly governs 2022 capital spending.","ruler_attribution":"High attribution through the presidential signature and formal budget/investment authority, though execution depended on line ministries and contractors.","contrary_evidence":["Budgeted allocation is not the same as completed delivery.","Inflation and exchange-rate volatility reduced the real value of allocations."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6"]}

4. 2023 investment program retrospective on 2022
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2023 Yılı Yatırım Programı Yayımlandı","publisher":"T.C. Cumhurbaşkanlığı Strateji ve Bütçe Başkanlığı","publication_date":"2023-01-15","url":"https://www.sbb.gov.tr/2023-yili-yatirim-programi-yayimlandi/","claim":"The 2023 investment-program article says 5,086 projects' planned investments were completed in 2022 and that the 2023 program added 3,607 new projects, with spending priorities concentrated in transport, mining, education, energy, agriculture and health.","locator":"lines 102-116","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official retrospective on completed 2022 projects plus forward 2023 program; strong for implementation evidence, though still state self-report.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on 2022 implementation, published at the start of 2023.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Erdoğan-led state apparatus and investment planning system, though project completion also reflects agency, contractor and inherited-pipeline effects.","contrary_evidence":["The article does not isolate Erdoğan's personal contribution from bureaucratic execution.","It reports completions, not independent evaluation of quality or cost overruns."],"lenses":["8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"]}

5. IMF mission on 2022 consultation
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Staff Completes 2022 Article IV Mission to Türkiye","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-11-04","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/11/04/pr22372-turkiye-imf-staff-completes-2022-article-iv-mission-to-turkiye","claim":"The IMF mission said policy-rate cuts in late 2021 added to existing vulnerabilities and were followed by lira depreciation and high inflation, with pressures partly relieved by FX interventions, the FX-protected deposit scheme, and macroprudential measures.","locator":"body text after the mission description","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent IMF staff assessment directly tied to 2022 policy developments; strong for implementation/outcome tracing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 policy performance, published in November 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly attributable to Erdoğan's chosen monetary framework and appointment/control over the economic team, though war and global prices also mattered.","contrary_evidence":["Some strain came from the Ukraine war and higher energy import prices.","The release also notes authorities used several defensive tools rather than only relying on rate cuts."],"lenses":["8B.3","8B.6","8B.8","8B.10"]}

6. IMF mission recommendations
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Staff Completes 2022 Article IV Mission to Türkiye","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-11-04","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/11/04/pr22372-turkiye-imf-staff-completes-2022-article-iv-mission-to-turkiye","claim":"The IMF recommended early policy-rate hikes, stronger central-bank independence, targeted assistance to vulnerable households, and the careful phasing out of FX-protected deposits and other macroprudential measures.","locator":"recommendations paragraph near the end of the press release","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Clear policy recommendation from an independent surveillance mission; strong as a counterfactual benchmark, not as a direct outcome measure.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly tied to the 2022 consultation and the policy outlook facing Erdoğan at year-end 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Highly relevant to Erdoğan because the recommendations addressed policies his administration controlled; the recommendation itself is IMF staff judgment.","contrary_evidence":["This is advice, not proof of what the ruler would or would not do.","Some measures had already been adopted, so the issue is completeness and direction of adjustment."],"lenses":["8B.8","8B.10"]}

7. OECD on early-2022 stabilization tools
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Türkiye: OECD Economic Outlook, Volume 2022 Issue 1","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-06-01","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-outlook/volume-2022/issue-1_62d0ca31-en/full-report/component-49.html","claim":"OECD said central-bank interventions and administrative measures, including requiring exporters to convert 40% of foreign-currency revenue into lira and the exchange-rate protection scheme for lira deposits, helped stabilize the exchange rate in early 2022, while fiscal policy remained supportive through wage increases and energy-consumer support.","locator":"line 4037 and surrounding text","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent OECD surveillance with concrete policy detail and near-real-time assessment of implementation effects.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers early 2022 implementation and market stabilization.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Erdoğan's policy line and the Treasury/central-bank apparatus under his control, though temporary stabilization also reflects coercive administrative tools and market conditions.","contrary_evidence":["The OECD notes exchange-rate weakness later returned.","The measures can stabilize markets without solving underlying inflation."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.3","8B.6","8B.8","8B.9"]}

8. OECD on late-2022 macro deterioration
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Türkiye: OECD Economic Outlook, Volume 2022 Issue 2","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-11-01","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-outlook/volume-2022/issue-2_f6da2159-en/full-report/component-51.html","claim":"OECD reported that consumer inflation reached 85.5% and producer inflation 158% by October 2022, that lira depreciation raised import-price pressures, and that monthly indicators suggested economic activity was decelerating.","locator":"line 4021 and surrounding text","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent late-2022 macro assessment with hard outcome indicators; strong for direction of results under the ruler's policy mix.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late 2022 outcome snapshot inside the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Substantially attributable to Erdoğan's chosen low-rate macro framework, though external energy and trade shocks contributed.","contrary_evidence":["External shocks from energy prices and the Ukraine war also mattered.","GDP growth remained positive overall, so the picture is deterioration rather than collapse."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.10"]}

9. OECD on household shielding
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Key policy insights: OECD Economic Surveys: Türkiye 2023","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-12-01","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-surveys-turkiye-2023_864ab2ba-en/full-report/component-4.html","claim":"OECD said the government paid 50% of households' electricity bills and 75% of natural gas bills, provided extra transfers to vulnerable households, raised the low-consumption electricity threshold, and lowered VAT on residential electricity and agricultural irrigation.","locator":"lines 4470-4474","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Concrete, multi-part description of implemented support measures from an independent OECD survey.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Implemented during 2022 and reported in the 2023 survey.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Erdoğan government as a crisis response mechanism under executive control, though delivery ran through fiscal and utility systems.","contrary_evidence":["The same survey warns of fiscal costs and contingent liabilities.","Broad price support can cushion pain without solving inflation."],"lenses":["8B.6","8B.9"]}

10. 2022 export record
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Yılı Dış Ticaret Rakamları Açıklandı","publisher":"T.C. Ticaret Bakanlığı","publication_date":"2023-01-02","url":"https://ticaret.gov.tr/haberler/2022-yili-dis-ticaret-rakamlari-aciklandi","claim":"Turkey's 2022 goods exports reached 254.2 billion dollars, up 12.9 percent from the previous year, and the ministry framed the result as a record achieved in difficult global conditions.","locator":"lines 157-172","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official year-end trade figures with a precise outcome measure; strong for output, though not a full causal attribution study.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on 2022 trade performance, published immediately after year-end.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Erdoğan's export-focused policy mix and currency conditions, but also affected by global prices, parity effects, and external demand.","contrary_evidence":["The ministry itself notes a favorable parity effect and strong global conditions around some sectors.","Exports alone do not prove broad policy competence."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.10"]}

11. TurkStat annual GDP release
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Annual Gross Domestic Product - 2022","publisher":"TURKSTAT Data Portal","publication_date":"2023-08-31","url":"https://veriportali.tuik.gov.tr/en/press/49742","claim":"TURKSTAT reported that annual GDP increased by 5.5 percent in 2022, GDP per capita reached 10,659 dollars, exports of goods and services rose 9.9 percent, and imports rose 8.6 percent.","locator":"press release summary on the data portal","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official statistical release from the national statistics office; strong for output and trade-growth outcomes, though not causal attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Official retrospective release for 2022 outcomes.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable in part to Erdoğan's policy setting, but also to tourism recovery, external demand, and base effects.","contrary_evidence":["High nominal growth coexisted with severe inflation and exchange-rate depreciation.","GDP averages can mask household-level strain."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.10"]}

12. NATO memorandum on Finland and Sweden
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Türkiye, Finland, and Sweden sign agreement paving the way for Finnish and Swedish NATO membership","publisher":"NATO","publication_date":"2022-06-28","url":"https://www.nato.int/en/news-and-events/articles/news/2022/06/28/turkiye-finland-and-sweden-sign-agreement-paving-the-way-for-finnish-and-swedish-nato-membership","claim":"In Madrid, Erdoğan and the Finnish and Swedish leaders agreed a trilateral memorandum addressing Türkiye's legitimate security concerns and paving the way for Finland and Sweden's NATO membership.","locator":"lines 813-815","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary NATO record of the agreement; strong evidence of diplomatic mobilization and concrete bargaining outcome.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly attributable to Erdoğan as Turkey's chief executive and foreign-policy decision-maker, though the final deal required counterparties and NATO facilitation.","contrary_evidence":["The agreement depended on multilateral bargaining, not unilateral Turkish control.","It opened a process rather than completing accession that year."],"lenses":["8B.3","8B.9","8B.10"]}

13. NATO follow-up on implementation
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press conference","publisher":"NATO","publication_date":"2022-10-20","url":"https://www.nato.int/en/news-and-events/events/transcripts/2022/10/20/press-conference","claim":"NATO said the Sweden-Finland accession process was the fastest in NATO's modern history and that Sweden was taking major concrete steps to implement the trilateral memorandum, including ending arms-sale restrictions and deepening counter-terrorism cooperation.","locator":"search result snippet","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"NATO transcript plus contemporaneous summary; strong evidence that the June bargain translated into follow-through during 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Follow-up implementation during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Erdoğan's bargaining and Türkiye's leverage, though the concrete follow-through also depended on Sweden, Finland and NATO procedures.","contrary_evidence":["The snippet reflects NATO's framing rather than Turkish self-report.","The process was still incomplete in 2022, so this is progress rather than completion."],"lenses":["8B.5","8B.9","8B.10"]}

14. Black Sea Grain Initiative agreement
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Note to correspondents on today’s agreements Istanbul","publisher":"United Nations in Ukraine","publication_date":"2022-07-22","url":"https://ukraine.un.org/en/191926-note-correspondents-today%E2%80%99s-agreements-istanbul","claim":"The UN said representatives from Ukraine, Russia and Türkiye signed an initiative in Istanbul that established a mechanism and a Joint Coordination Centre to safely transport grain, foodstuffs and fertilizer from Ukrainian ports to global markets and to prevent global hunger.","locator":"lines 69-74 and 88-96","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN description of the agreement, mechanism and purpose; strong evidence of diplomatic execution and crisis management.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly attributable to Erdoğan and the Turkish government as hosts and brokers, though success required Russian, Ukrainian and UN participation.","contrary_evidence":["This was a multilateral arrangement, not a unilateral Turkish achievement.","Its success was contingent on war-time compliance and shipping security."],"lenses":["8B.3","8B.9"]}

15. Black Sea Grain Initiative early progress
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2.7 million metric tons of grain and other foodstuffs were moved from Ukraine under Black Sea Grain Initiative","publisher":"United Nations in Türkiye","publication_date":"2022-09-13","url":"https://turkiye.un.org/en/198923-27-million-metric-tons-grain-and-other-foodstuffs-were-moved-ukraine-under-black-sea-grain","claim":"By 12 September 2022, 2.7 million metric tons of grain and other foodstuffs had moved through the Black Sea Grain Initiative, and the Joint Coordination Centre in Istanbul was monitoring vessel movements and revising corridor procedures.","locator":"lines 59-70","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN progress report with a concrete throughput figure and operational details; strong evidence of implementation and early results.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and only weeks after the agreement.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable in part to Erdoğan's successful brokering and Türkiye's hosting of the JCC, though the throughput depended on all signatories and shipping compliance.","contrary_evidence":["The initiative was not solely Turkish-owned.","Its durability depended on continuing wartime cooperation."],"lenses":["8B.6","8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I screened roughly 30 documents across IMF, OECD, World Bank, NATO, UN, Turkish Presidency/Strateji-Bütçe, TurkStat, the Trade Ministry, Reuters, and EBRD coverage. I kept the sources that showed a real chain from program to implementation to outcome, and dropped repeated Reuters rewrites of the same low-rate story, generic country-level macro indicators with no ruler-specific mechanism, and older 2021 precursor pieces unless they were needed to explain a 2022 action. I also treated broad post-2022 retrospectives as usable only when they explicitly summarized 2022 implementation or outcomes. A few Turkish official pages were only accessible as search snippets in this environment, so I used those snippets rather than forcing weaker duplicates.

**Remaining gaps**
The dossier is strongest on Erdoğan's macroeconomic program, investment planning, crisis-response measures, and a couple of foreign-policy wins. The main gap is direct, fine-grained evidence of 2022 implementation quality inside line ministries, procurement, or audit records, especially anything that would show whether specific projects were completed on time and within cost. More search would help if you want a tighter competence judgment on budget execution, project delivery, or whether personnel changes inside the economic team materially improved implementation.
